## When to invoke

- Adding, changing, or removing API endpoints / RPC handlers.
- Data models, schema design, migrations, query work.
- Authentication, authorization, session and token handling.
- Background jobs, queues, scheduled work, integrations with external services.
- Server-side validation, serialization, error shaping at the API boundary.

## API design

- Versioning matters at the contract layer. Breaking changes need a new version path or a deprecation cycle — never silently change response shape.
- Endpoints describe resources (`POST /users/:id/avatar`), not verbs (`POST /uploadUserAvatar`). RPC-style action endpoints are fine for genuine actions (`POST /orders/:id/cancel`).
- Response shape is a contract. Adding fields is safe; renaming or removing is breaking. Document the difference in the changelog.
- Pagination on any endpoint that returns a list. Default + max page size. Cursor for large datasets, offset is fine for small ones.
- Idempotency where it's possible — `PUT`/`DELETE`/`POST` with an idempotency key. Document non-idempotent endpoints explicitly.

## Validation and error shaping

- Validate every input crossing the API boundary: body, query, params, headers. A validation library (zod, joi, pydantic, etc.) over hand-written checks.
- Invalid input → 400 with the validation detail. Auth failure → 401. Permission failure → 403. Resource missing → 404. Conflict → 409. Server error → 500. Don't reuse 400 for everything.
- Error responses have a stable shape. `{ error, code, details? }` or similar — never raw strings, never stack traces in the body.
- Validation errors include enough detail to fix without round-tripping: which field, what was wrong. Not just "invalid request".

## Persistence

- Parameterized queries always. String concatenation with user input is the entry-level mistake.
- N+1 is a real failure mode, not a theoretical one. If you're calling the ORM inside a loop, look at it.
- Transactions wrap operations that must atomically succeed together. Don't rely on "it usually works".
- Migrations are forward-only in production. If you need to undo, you write a forward migration that undoes it. "Drop column" migrations need a deprecation period — `ADD column, dual-write, backfill, switch reads, drop` is four migrations, not one.
- Indexes have a cost on writes. Don't add one without a query that needs it; don't keep one without a query that uses it.

## Authentication and authorization

- Authentication: are you who you say you are. Authorization: are you allowed to do this. Two different checks at two different layers.
- Authorization belongs at the data layer (or a layer just above it), not only at the route. A user with a direct DB connection should still be unable to read other users' rows.
- Object-level permissions, not just role-level. "User can edit posts" needs to also check "this post belongs to this user."
- Session tokens are HTTPOnly + Secure + SameSite. JWT in localStorage is XSS-readable.
- Never trust client-supplied identity. `userId` in a request body for a "who am I editing as" check is a bug, not a feature.

## Background work

- Idempotent jobs. A retried job must not double-charge, double-send, double-anything.
- Jobs that can fail need a visible failure path — dead-letter queue, alert, ticket. Silently dropped failures are how data goes missing.
- Don't block API requests on slow work. Enqueue and return. The user's connection is not your job queue.
- Schedule timing is documented. "Runs daily" means nothing in an incident at 3am.

## External integrations

- Treat every external API call as potentially slow, potentially failing. Timeouts, retries with backoff, circuit breakers where the dependency is critical.
- Idempotency keys when the external API supports them — your retries must not double-charge.
- Webhook receivers verify signatures. Replay protection (nonces or timestamps) prevents an old payload from being re-played.
- Log enough context to debug without re-running the call. Request ID, status, latency, redacted payload size.

## Observability

- Structured logs (JSON) in production. The schema is documented somewhere.
- Every error path emits the request context needed to diagnose: request ID, user ID (when known), endpoint, error type. Not just "something went wrong".
- Don't log secrets, tokens, full request bodies, or PII. A redaction layer beats discipline.

## Out of scope

- UI components, browser behavior — defer to the frontend agent.
- Infrastructure provisioning, deploy pipelines — defer to the devops agent.
- Cryptographic protocol design — defer to the security reviewer.
